title: What the week actually cost
subtitle: week-23 - Cost Explorer, 14-18 Oct 2026, grouped by usage type
width: 1120
---
$ aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-14,End=2026-10-19 \
      --granularity DAILY --metrics UnblendedCost UsageQuantity \
      --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=USAGE_TYPE

  USE1-VPCLattice-Service-Hourly        21 hrs  15 Oct     $0.525000
  USE1-VPCLattice-Service-Hourly        19 hrs  16 Oct     $0.475000
  USE1-VPCLattice-RequestCount-Free     35 requests        $0.000000
  USE1-VPCLattice-DataProcessing-Bytes                     $0.000001
                                                        -----------
                                                           $1.000001

# 40 hours at $0.025. The service was created at 22:11 on the 14th and
# destroyed at 15:20 on the 16th; AWS folded the first evening's hours
# into the 16th rather than posting them against the 14th.

--- what is NOT in this number ------------------------------------------

Lambda and CloudWatch for this week fell inside the free tier. Their usage
types cannot be separated from the rest of the account -- Lambda-GB-Second
and Request keep accruing on the 17th and 18th, after everything here was
destroyed -- so attributing any part of them to this week would be a guess.

The Lattice lines are unambiguous: nothing else in the account uses it.

--- the shape of the bill ------------------------------------------------

  99.9999% of it            the service existing, per hour
  0.0001%                   data through it
  0%                        the 35 requests the tests made

A Lattice service bills for being reachable, not for being called. An idle
service network costs exactly what a busy one does. The variable that moves
this number is how long it is left standing, which is why the week ends
with a destroy run and not a quiet weekend.
